\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\kerning2\b\f0\fs28 The WSPR Scheduler App (and associated tools)\par
\fs24 What is WSPR Scheduler?\line\b0 WSPR scheduler is an app that allows Weak Signal Position Reporter (WSPR) timeslots to be scheduled. Each slot lasts 2 minutes and can be used to either transmit WSPR beacon signals or receive them off air from other WSPR stations.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa120\sl276\slmult1 There are four main tools currently available within the WSPR Scheduler suite of apps:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa120\sl276\slmult1 the WSPR Scheduler itself, which includes encoding software to transmit a WSPR signal, consisting of callsign, position locator and power level within a two minutes timeslot and also links to a decoder to receive, decode and display received WSPR transmissions. Frequency selection is automatic for each timeslot using the hamlib library. Antenna selection can also be automated by automatic switching according to the antenna configured for each individual timeslot - therefore if you have multiple antennas covering different bands you do not need to switch antennas manually and antenna comparison can be automated. Operation of an auto tuner is also possible.\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}WSPR live reporter. This links to wspr.live to retrieve reports of own station WSPR transmissions from receiving stations around the World and displays the decoded transmissions.\par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}WSPR Map - this displays the reported transmissions on a World map to show the location of receiving stations that have successfully received own call transmissions as well as the location of stations received by own station.\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}WSPR Sked Solar (WS_Solar)- this retrieves solar geomagnetic data from NOAA in the US and also the two GOES satellites. This data is stored and displayed for current and future recording of propagation information.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\par
\fs28 Installing WSPR Scheduler\par
\b0\fs24 Before any features of WSPR Scheduler are configured the databases must be setup and configured. It is recommended that xampp be used to run the databases, though MySQL Community can be used to run MySQL. The following instructions are for xampp, which are the most straightforward method of installing and running the databases (instructions for the MySQL Community edition are given later in the appendix). First we\rquote ll install WSPR Scheduler, but we can\rquote t configure it until we have installed the databases \endash  however, the database file wspr_db.sql will be made available when we do this.\par
\par
\par
\b Step 1\tab\tab Install Hamlib for Rig Control\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 From Github or Sourceforge download Hamlib \endash  see {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://sourceforge.net/projects/hamlib/ }}{\fldrslt{https://sourceforge.net/projects/hamlib/\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}Install Rig control to the recommendaed folder.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b Step 2\tab\tab Install WSPR Scheduler\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 Download and install WSPR_Sked.msi\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}Download the WSPR Scheduler file with the msi extension from Sourceforge (eg. WSPRSked.msi) - see {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://sourceforge.net/projects/wspr-scheduler/files/ }}{\fldrslt{https://sourceforge.net/projects/wspr-scheduler/files/\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24\par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}Then select the msi installer for WSPR Scheduler (WSPRSked.msi) and run it \endash  installing it for all users.\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}You can start WSPR Scheduler, but it will warn you that the databases aren\rquote t installed and will quit \endash  \ul that is normal\ulnone .\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 However \endash  a folder called C:\\WSPR_Sked will be created and a \b wspr_db.sql\b0  file will appear in there \endash  that contains the databases to be imported\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 So let\rquote s install and configure the databases!\par
\par
\b Step 43\tab Database Configuration\par
\b0 Before running and configuring WSPR Scheduler you will need to install the databases that are hosted in MySQL. You can use xampp to run the MySQL databases (instructions on using the alternative MySQL Community are given in the appendix). The follwijg instruction are for Xampp:\par
Note: more detailed information on the databases and installation options are given in the document How-to-Database-Configuration.pdf.\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Download xampp from: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://www.apachefriends.org/download.html }}{\fldrslt{https://www.apachefriends.org/download.html\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}Select the Windows version.\par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}Install this as an administrator on your PC\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The file \b wspr_db.sql\b0  containing the database definitions will be downloaded and installed in the \b C:\\WSPR_Sked\b0  folder when you install WSPR_Sked.msi\par
- otherwise, if for some reason it isn\rquote t there, download the wspr_db.sql file, create the C:\\WSPR_Sked folder and copy it into there. (you can locate the initial database file from Sourceforge at: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://sourceforge.net/projects/wspr-scheduler/files/ }}{\fldrslt{https://sourceforge.net/projects/wspr-scheduler/files/\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24 )\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 4)\tab Start the xampp control panel\par
5)\tab Select \b Admin\b0  next to MySQL\par
6)\tab When phpMyAdmin opens select \b Import \b0 (from the top menu)\par
7)\tab Under \ldblquote File to Import\rdblquote  select \b Browse\par
\b0 8)\tab Locate the file \b wspr_db.sql\b0\par
9)\tab Then at the bottom of the page select \b Import\b0\par
10)\tab Then select \b User Accounts \b0 (from the top menu)\par
11)\tab Then \b Add user account\par
\b0 12)\tab In the User name field enter: \tab\b admin\b0\par
13)\tab In the Password field enter: \tab\b wspr\par
\b0 14)\tab confirm this by entering again\par
15)\tab Under \b Global Privileges\b0  select \b Check All\b0\par
16)\tab The scroll down to hit \b Go\b0\par
17)\tab Confirm the addition by selecting User Accounts again\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Note: these are the default username and password for the wspr databases and the password can be changed later\par
Hint: you may wish to add a password for the root account to improve security\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 18)\tab To ensure the database starts at system startup go back to the xampp control panel\par
19)\tab select \b Config\par
\b0 20)\tab Under \b Autostart\b0  of modules select \b MySQL\b0  (and also Apache if you want to administer the database with phpmyadmin)\par
21)\tab ..... it is a good idea to check "Start Control Panel Minimized" - this will ensure that mysql is started when you login to Windows\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-1440\li1440\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Note:  i)\tab if you get access denied then you will need to start the xampp control panel as an administrator (right click on it and select "Run as administrator".\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-720\li1440\sa160\sl276\slmult1 ii)\tab you may find that when you restart Windows you still have to open the xampp control panel to start MySQL.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 You can now install Wspr Scheduler and the associated applications if you already haven\rquote t done so.\par
\b Step 4 \tab Installing WSPR Scheduler\par
\b0 If you haven\rquote t already done so you should install WSPR Scheduler now (see step 2)\par
\b Step 5\tab\tab Installing Optional tools\par
\b0 There are three other tools that have been developed alongside WSPR Scheduler to support its operation: \par
WSPR Sked Live - displays reception reports from other stations of your transmissions that were uploaded to wsprnet and relayed to wspr.live\par
WSPR Map - displays a map of reports on own call (this relies on the data recorded by WSPR Sked live)\par
WSPR Sked Solar (WS_Solar) - records and displays solar and propagation data and reports from NOAA\par
Download and install these in the same way as you did for WSPR Scheduler itself.\par
\b Step 6\tab\tab Starting and Configuring WSPR Scheduler\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 Start WSPR Scheduler (WSPR_Sked) as an administrator (as you will need to make system changes when you configure and run it)\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\fs28 TX Configuration:\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 2)\tab\fs24 Select the TX Config tab\par
3)\tab Enter your callsign \endash  this may include a prefix or suffix, but if it does you will need to use type 2 or 3 messages to send WSPR transmissions. Plain calls just require type 1 messages.\par
4)\tab When you hit the enter key the base callsign box should automatically be filled with the base call, excluding prefix/suffixes. You may need to edit this if the base call is incorrect.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The following values are defaults that can be changed when a WSPR transmission slot is configured:\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 5)\tab Enter the default offset value between 0 to 200 Hz. WSPR transmitters normally use an audio modulation signal (in SSB or data mode) of between 1400 to 1600Hz. The base frequency is 1400Hz \endash  so you should add an offset of between 0 to 200Hz to specify where within the WSPR transmission passband to transmit (1400-1600Hz). \par
6)\tab Select the default transmitter power in dBm, between 0 and 60 (that is 1mW to 1 kW). \par
7)\tab Select a default WSPR frequency. \par
8)\tab Select a default antenna. At this stage you may be presented with the values preconfigured in the database or none at all. You will need to configure your own antennas and then come back to select the default later.\par
9)\tab Enter your position as a 4 digit Maidenhead locator. If you enter a 6 digit value and intend to use this in WSPR messages you will need to select the checkbox to enable 6 digit locators and you will need to ensure that type 2/3 messages are enabled. A 4 digit locator and a standard callsign are sent in type 1 messages.\par
10)\tab When sending type 2/3 messages an external encoder is used, wspr_enc.exe. This is normally located in the C:\\WSPR_Sked folder on installation, but can be changed if necessary.\par
11)\tab If you want to allow type 2/3 messages and have configured the path above select allow type 2/3 messages. Many people send them as a single message where a hashing algorithm encodes the call \endash  so select this box too (if you don\rquote t select it then type 2/3 messages are sent in 2 parts \endash  the first a type 1 and the second a type 2/3).\par
12)\tab Select Save Settings once you have configured or made changes to the options.\par
13)\tab When you setup your locator or change location by a significant distance, usually more than 40 miles (though 100 miles won\rquote t make a significant difference), you need to create/update the sunrise and sunset times. This interrogates api.sunrise-sunset.org and stores the sunrise/set times in the local database.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl276\slmult1\par
Additional options:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1080\sa160\sl276\slmult1 If running in TX only mode, for example when portable, you can disable decoding of received WSPR signals (this also closes the form that displays received stations).\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}You can also change the default admin user password \endash  this will be applied to all databases. Select Save Password once you have made the change.\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Adjusting the Gaussian filter. Without a gaussian filter the transitions between tones in the WSPR transmissions would increase the bandwidth of WSPR transmissions. Applying a gaussian filter smooths these transitions and reduces the bandwidth of the signal. A high filter value may reduce bandwidth, but the signal may exhibit a noticeable wobble in amplitude, though a lower value increases the bandwidth. So maybe its best left to curiosity \endash  the default value is 0.05.\par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}There are various tests that can be conducted \endash  they are self-explanatory, but more of this later.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\fs28 Rig (transceiver) Configuration\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0\fs24 Select the Rigctl rigs tab\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}If you are not using rigtcl then select the \ldblquote do not use rigctld\rdblquote  box. This means that you will have to manually change the frequency of your WSPR transmitter and may have to use VOX to start transmissions. This may apply if your rig is not in the list mentioned below.\par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}However if you are using rigctld to control your transceiver select the correct path to the rigctld.exe daemon (the default is C:\\WSPR_Sked, but if Hamlib is installed you can find it in the C:\\Program Files\\hamlib-w64-4.6.5\\bin folder, but note that your version may have a different number).\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}You will also need to select the IP address of the PC on which rigctld is running \endash  usually its this PC \endash  in which case enter 127.0.0.1 and the port number \endash  we have set this to 4532, but check this.\par
{\pntext\f0 5)\tab}Select your rig from the long list available for use with rigctld.\par
{\pntext\f0 6)\tab}If using CAT control instead of VOX then select the COM port and the baud rate shown to the left hand side.\par
{\pntext\f0 7)\tab}Once you have made the change save them by selecting \i Save Rigctl Config\par
{\pntext\f0 8)\tab}\i0 Start the rigctl daemon\i\par

\pard\widctlpar\li270\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\i0\fs28 RX and Sound Config\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0\fs24 Select the RX & Sound config tab.\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}The wsprd.exe decoder is normally installed in the C:\\WSPR_Sked directory so you don\rquote t need to change this, however, you can also place it in a different folder, or if WSJT-X is installed then you can access it from the bin folder there (eg. C:\\WSJT\\wsjtx\\bin). \par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}Select the audio output device (eg. the speaker output of your sound card) \endash  this will be the audio from your PC to the transmitter.\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}Select the audio input device (either the mic or line in of your sound card) \endash  this will be audio from your receiver into the PC.\par
{\pntext\f0 5)\tab}Fine tune the mic/line level when you are receiving a signal (you don\rquote t need to do this initially). If you are receiving well then the vertical bar should be at about the half way vertical point (if it is very low then you may not be receiving audio correctly from your receiver/transceiver).\par
{\pntext\f0 6)\tab}Save the Settings\par

\pard\widctlpar\li270\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\fs28 Frequencies and Antennas\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0\fs24 Select the Freq & Ant tab\par

\pard\widctlpar\li270\sa160\sl276\slmult1\ul Frequencies:\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1\ulnone 2)\tab Ensure that the WSPR frequency list is fully populated with the main WSPR frequencies. Whilst you can edit and add to these only the standard ones are currently supported in any case.\par
3)\tab When you are transmitting (you can do this from the TX Config tab Test TX PTT button) you can fine tune the audio output fed to the transmitter to effectively reduce power or to prevent audio levels that allow the ALC to kick in). This is useful if you wish to adjust power output on different bands without having to do this manually each time.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Notes: on fine tuning the audio level here:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1440\sl276\slmult1 Set the maximum level that you want to transmit on your transmitter \endash  say 10 Watts.\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}On one or more bands set the audio level here in WSPR scheduler to 1.0.\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Then transmit (ideally into a dummy load) and monitor the power level on an accurate SWR/power meter.\par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}Set the audio output from your sound card (eg. the speaker output) so that it just puts the power indicated on the SWR/power meter to the value that you set on the TX (eg. 10W) \endash  this volume setting may only be 5 out of 100 or less. It is best to use a variable audio attenuator in line with the audio to fine tune the audio level adjustment.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li1440\sl276\slmult1 Tip: installing an inline volume control/ variable audio attenuator (connected between speaker output on the PC and the audio to the TX) will help with fine adjustment of the audio level.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-720\li1440\sl276\slmult1 v)\tab Here in WSPR Scheduler - on each band finely adjust the audio output as necessary on each band.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li1440\sl276\slmult1 If you wish to transmit with the full power that you set on the TX then you can leave the audio level at or close to 1 on each band, but if you want to transmit on some bands with a lower power (say 5W) then reduce the audio level when transmitting on each of those bands so that the power meter indicates the reduced power level (this setting may typically be between 0.5 to 0.7 in WSPR scheduler).\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\ul Antennas:\par
\ulnone Note: the switches and tuners mentioned here are configured from the Switches&Tuners tab.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1 4)\tab Under Antennas add each of your antennas add a name and description of each. \par
5)\tab When editing each antenna you are prompted for an antenna switch and a tuner for each one. If you are switching antennas and tuning them manually you should select none. \par

\pard\widctlpar\li270\sa160\sl276\slmult1 However, one of the most important features of WSPR Scheduler is that it can automate the switching and tuning of antennas for each WSPR transmission and band. So you can use an automatic antenna switch (examples are an Arduino operated switch or a W410 antenna switch is also supported). More than one switch can be selected for multiple antennas. \par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1 6)\tab If you are using an antenna switch select the switch and the port on that switch to which the antenna is connected. \par
7)\tab Select a tuner if you are using one.\par
8)\tab Save the settings for the antennas once you have edited them.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 A second switch can also be selected \endash  this allows a remote antenna switch to be operated in series with the first one. For example, whilst you may have antennas from various sources or directions converging on your shack you may wish to have multiple antennas sharing a single cable (eg. coaxial cable) \endash  in which case you could switch these antennas to the single cable by using a remote antenna switch.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li630\sa160\sl276\slmult1 9)\tab If you wish to select a remote (or second) antenna switch - select the switch number and the port to which the antenna is connected as you did previously.\par
10)\tab Save the settings.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\fs28 Antenna Switches and Tuners\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0\fs24 Select the Switches&Tuners tab.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Up to 10 automatic antenna switches and 10 antenna tuners are supported.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 2)\tab Select a switch from the list and edit it.\par
3)\tab Select the method that you will use to communicate with the switch: Serial (RS232), TCP or UDP.\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li2160\sa160\sl276\slmult1 If using Serial: select com port, baud rate, data bits, parity, stop bits and flow control appropriate to your switch (eg. for a W410)\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}If using TCP (or UDP) select the IP address of the switch, port number, (eg. 5000) and IP address (eg. for a wirelessly connected Arduino based switch).\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 4)\tab Select the switch type \endash  at the moment an Arduino switch or a W410 are supported. Both types have been successfully tested. \par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Note: ideally house the antenna switch itself in a shielded metal box.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 5)\tab Enter the number of antenna ports on the switch.\par
6)\tab Select Save to save the settings.\par
7)\tab Do the same thing if using a tuner (currently you can use a tuner that is triggered by a single switch). This hasn\rquote t been tested yet.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b Note: options for automatic antenna switches:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 the Weytoll W-410A 200W Automatic Antenna Switch in use at G4GCI is available from Ebay or Amazon (cost between \'a3140 to \'a3200). This is controlled from the PC USB port. \par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}alternatively an AT-14 four way automatic antenna switch (in a metal case) is available from Amazon, Ebay or Ali Express for \'a340 or more. This is manually switched but can be connected to an Arduino to control the switching operation from within WSPR Scheduler (via Wifi). This has been tested and operates well at G4GCI - see diagrams and Arduino code in Files section.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Now you are ready to start configuring WSPR transmission slots.\par
\b Hint: TCP/UDP Connected Switches\par
\b0 First you will need to find the IP address that your wireless router has issued wirelessly to the Arduino to which the swich is connected. If you use an AT-14 switch the Arduino sketch for an R4 wifi is shown in the \ldblquote Antenna Switches\rdblquote  folder on Sourceforge. If you connect this to a USB port on a PC and find its COM port you can use Putty or the Arduino IDE to connect to it (9600 bps) and when you enter \ldblquote status\rdblquote  you will see the IP address. Other ways to find the IP address are to look for the esp32 device in the connected client list on your router. You should go to the DHCP or DHCP Server section on your router and add this device and its IP address to the address reservations list so that the Arduino always receives the same IP address when you switch it on, otherwise you will be unable to contact the antenna switch if the address changes.\par
\b\par
\fs28 Timeslot Window\par
\b0\fs24 When WSPR Scheduler opens you are presented with the timeslot window. This shows a list of timeslots. The information contained in each row is as follows:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1080\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Timeslot date and time\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}Frequency to be used in the timeslot\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Offset in Hertz between 0-200 (offset from the base audio of 1400Hz)\par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}Power in dBm and watts for the timeslot \par
{\pntext\f0 v)\tab}Antenna to be used \par
{\pntext\f0 vi)\tab}Tuner and antenna switch number if used\par
{\pntext\f0 vii)\tab}End date of the slot if a repeating slot\par
{\pntext\f0 viii)\tab}Whether the slot is a repeating slot (ticked if so)\par
{\pntext\f0 ix)\tab}Repeat type (Rtype). Slots can be repeated over a period of time. Ther are 5 options for repeating slots each hour: 1. between the slot time and a time up to midnight, 2. between sunrise to sunset, 3. between sunset and sunrise, 4. over 24 hours, or just repeat the slot at the same time each day (type 0). \endash  the repeating slot column will be ticked if any of these options are chosen.\par
{\pntext\f0 x)\tab}If the slot repeats: if it repeats at the same time every day \endash  \ldblquote 1hr\rdblquote , or the end time, or \ldblquote Night\rdblquote  if it runs from sunset-sunrise, \ldblquote Day\rdblquote  if from sunrise-sunset or \ldblquote 24hrs\rdblquote . If it doesn\rquote t repeat, then \ldblquote once\rdblquote .\par
{\pntext\f0 xi)\tab}Greyline offset (Goff) \endash  if repeat between sunrise-sunset or sunset-sunrise is chosen then an offset in minutes (between 0 to 180) can be chosen (eg. 30 minutes before or after sunset/sunrise)\par
{\pntext\f0 xii)\tab}Whether the slot is active (active = TX slot, not active = RX slot)\par
{\pntext\f0 xiii)\tab}Slot number (always 1 unless type 2/3 message type is chosen to be sent as two transmissions, in which case the second transmission will be slot 2)\par
{\pntext\f0 xiv)\tab}Message type (normally 1, but may be 2 or 3 if compound calls/6 digit locator used in the WSPR transmission.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Note: The date and time of a slot can be selected from the date picker and time list on the left hand side. To select the current time select the time now button.\par
To allow the slot display to follow the current time \endash  select Track slots (this stops temporarily when you are making changes on the page).\par
\b Configuring a WSPR Transmission slot.\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 Select the slots tab.\par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}Select a date and time that you wish to configure a slot for \endash  make sure that the slot (row) that you wish to configure is highlighted.\par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}Double click the left mouse button on the slot in the right hand margin of the slot list.\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}Use the default values for frequency, offset (in Hz), power (dBm), antenna \endash  or change them as appropriate.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Note: the power selection doesn\rquote t change the power on the transceiver from here \endash  you will need to do this manually (or adjust the audio from the Freq&Ant tab).\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The slot can be set as a single slot at this date/time or can be setup to repeat at the same time each hour as follows:\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 5)\tab If you want to allow the slot to repeat select \ldblquote repeating timeslot \'85\rdblquote\par
6)\tab Select the date that you want the slot to repeat until (leave it as today if just one day)\par
7)\tab You have five options for allowing the slot to repeat:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1440\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Just select an end date, but not end time or the other options \endash  means that the slot is repeated at the same time each day (repeat type 0)\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}Select the \ldblquote repeat for same minutes each hour \'85\rdblquote  and select the end time (the slot will repeat between the current selected time and the end time that you select),\tab (repeat type 1)\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Select \ldblquote repeat for the same time each hour from sunrise until sunset \'85\rdblquote  \endash  if you want the slot to be repeat during daylight hours each day (useful for higher HF bands). Also select an offset in minutes (so if you select 60 the timeslot will repeat between sunrise minus 60 minutes and sunset plus 60 minutes approximately) \endash  0 just means from sunrise to sunset. (repeat type 2)\par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}Select \ldblquote repeat for same time each hour between sunset and sunrise \'85\rdblquote  \endash  if you want the slot to repeat during hours of darkness (useful for lower HF bands). Also select an offset in minutes (so if you select 60 the timeslot will repeat between midnight and sunrise plus 60 minutes and again from sunset minus 60 minutes to midnight) \endash  0 just means no offset from sunrise/sunset. (repeat type 3)\par
{\pntext\f0 v)\tab}Select \ldblquote repeat for same minute \'85 over 24hours\rdblquote  \endash  if you want the slot to be repeated at the same time each hour over 24 hours over the selected date range (useful for mid-range frequencies). (repeat type 4)\par
{\pntext\f0 vi)\tab}To activate the transmitter to send a WSPR message during this slot check the \ldblquote activate\rdblquote  option. To make it a receive only slot (in which received stations will be recorded and decoded) leave the box unchecked.\par
{\pntext\f0 vii)\tab}Once you are ready select \ldblquote Save slot\rdblquote .\par
{\pntext\f0 viii)\tab}This slot and any repeating slots will be updated.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 In the slot display window you will notice that the slot repeat type (Rtype) and the grey offset (Goff) are also shown\par
Note: if you schedule slots to between sunrise-sunset, or vice versa, or over 24 hours \endash  avoid trying to schedule the slots over a period of more than two months at a time as the processing time required can be quite slow. (further work will look at ways to speed this up).\par
\b Editing slots\par
\b0 To edit an existing slot just double click the left mouse button in the right-hand margin in the same way \endash  changes can then be made to this slot and you will be prompted to update any repeating slots too when the changes are saved.\par
\b Deleting slots\par
\b0 To delete a slot right click on the right-hand margin and select Delete. If the slot is repeating you will also be prompted to decide whether to delate all associated repeating slots too.\par
You can also delete a whole range of slots by selecting \ldblquote delete all slots from \ldblquote  and then highlighting a date range from the date picker before deleting them.\par
\b Start Transmitting\par
\b0 Select Enable TX in the slots tab or check the Enable TX button in the TX Config tab. The button should turn from green to red to indicate that the transmitter is ready to transmit.\par
When the next scheduled timeslot arrives the transmitter will be activated and the antenna switched to the appropriate antenna.\par
\b Time Synchronization\par
\b0 If you notice that slots are being missed or are running late it may be a timing issue.\par
If the system clock is adrift by more than one or two seconds this may result in WSPR slots being missed or transmitting too late. Make sure that the system clock is kept up to date regularly. You need to make sure that the Windows Time Service is running and the system clock is correct. If you have a smartphone with an Internet synchronised clock you can also check it against this. \par
The latest version of WSPR Scheduler checks the system time against an Internet time source at 03.15 and 15.15 UTC every day (and starts Windows Time Service if it isn\rquote t started). If WSPR Sked is running with Administrator privileges it will update the time, if not it will warn you if the time is incorrect by >1 second.\par
You can also update or check the clock by hitting the \ldblquote\b sync clock\b0\rdblquote  button, which runs a time synchronisation.\par
Other tips for clock synchronisation: \par

\pard 
{\listtext\f0 a.\tab}\ls1\ilvl1\widctlpar\fi-360\li1440\sl276\slmult1 From a command prompt (enter \ldblquote cmd\rdblquote  at Windows start) and run as administrator \endash  enter: \tab\b net start w32time\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\li1440\sl276\slmult1 This will ensure that the Windows Time Service is running\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li1440\sl276\slmult1 b.\tab Then enter: \tab\tab\b w32tm /resync /force\b0  \par

\pard\widctlpar\li1440\sl276\slmult1 This will force an update. (if you get an error you can update the clock in Windows \endash  see below)\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 You can also start the time service from Windows:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1440\sl276\slmult1 Press the Windows button (bottom left of keyboard) + R\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}type \b services.msc\b0  and hit Enter\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Find "Windows Time"\par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}Right-click \f1\u8594?\f0\lang2057  Properties\par
{\pntext\f0 v)\tab}Set Startup type to "Automatic"\par
{\pntext\f0 vi)\tab}Click Start\par
{\pntext\f0 vii)\tab}Click OK\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 You can also update the time now - right click on the time/date to the bottom right of the display in Windows. Then select \ldblquote Adjust date/time\rdblquote . Ensure that \ldblquote Set the time automatically\rdblquote  is on and then select \ldblquote Sync now\rdblquote\par
\b\par
Selecting an Alternative Slot Database\par
\b0 Two slot databases can be selected. The main slot database is stored in the wspr_slots database. However, an option at the bottom of the slot display enables an alternative database to be selected. This means that if you have a regular schedule and you want to make some tests without having to modify it you can select the alternative (or \ldblquote test\rdblquote ) database \endash  which is stored in the wspr_slots_test database.\par
To select the test database hit the button next to \ldblquote slot database in use\rdblquote  at the bottom of the slots display. You will initially be presented with a blank schedule that you can populate.\par
To switch back to the main schedule hit the button again.\par
When you exit WSPR Scheduler and you save the settings the currently selection will be saved.\par
\b\par
Testing\par
\b0 To run TX tests select the TX Configuration tab. \par
Select a frequency and antenna.\par
To just run a short TX test \endash  hit Test TX/PTT\par
To start a non-scheduled WSPR transmission select Wspr TX Test \endash  the transmission will start at the next even minute.\par
You can also test the audio \endash  monitor the output that will be fed to your TX. It is difficult to tell whether the audio tones are changing as per the encoded sequence \endash  so you can also select the option to use wider tones so that you hear that the tones are changing \endash  switch this off before actually transmitting.\par
Other tests allow you test connection to an Arduino and you can also test the WSPR TX encoding.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs28 Receiving WSPR Signals\par
\b0\fs24 Included with the WSPR Schedule is a panel that displays received WSPR decodes when the transceiver is not transmitting. It is possible to switch this off by selecting the Stop RX/decoding button in the TX Configuration tab in the main WSPR Scheduler window (this may be useful when portable and only wishing to transmit WSPR messages, but not receive them).\par
This panel displays the following:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1080\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Date/time of reception, \par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}the callsign of the received station, \par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}frequency on which received, receive signal to noise ratio (SNR), \par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}any drift noticed on the received signal, \par
{\pntext\f0 v)\tab}power level of the transmitting station in dBm, \par
{\pntext\f0 vi)\tab}their location as a Maidenhead locator (4 or 6 digits)\par
{\pntext\f0 vii)\tab}the distance to the received station in km and miles \par
{\pntext\f0 viii)\tab} and also the direction that the station was received from (short path) \endash  its azimuth in degrees.\par
{\pntext\f0 ix)\tab}a calculation based on the received SNR of the expected signal strength of a call on both CW and SSB at a selected power level\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The data is recorded in the wspr_rpt database (in the received table)\par
The displayed information can be filtered by:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1080\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Date/time range\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}Individual or all bands\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Call sign (including wildcards such as VK*)\par
{\pntext\f0 iv)\tab}Minimum and maximum distance between the station and our receiver (in km or miles) (this is useful for filtering out a lot of calls that are close to our station)\par
{\pntext\f0 v)\tab}The power of a CW/SSB transmitter used to calculate expected reception using these modes based on the SNR of received WSPR stations.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 These filter options can be applied with the Apply button and cleared again with the Clear button. \par
Other options include selection of additional, search methods when decoding the received WSPR transmission as follows:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 i)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcrm{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-720\li1080\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Deep search (to provide a more thorough search for weaker signals)\par
{\pntext\f0 ii)\tab}quick search if speed is required (for example on slower computers)\par
{\pntext\f0 iii)\tab}Overlapped Segment Decoder (OSD) depth. A higher level of 5 allows more sensitive as it allows a deeper search for weaker WSPR signals, whilst a lower level of 1 is less sensitive with a lower depth of search. Deeper searching is much slower than more shallow searching.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Spots can be uploaded to the WSPRnet web site by checking the Upload spots box.\par
If you are filtering the results each decode may interrupt the search \endash  so you can switch off receiving temporarily by selecting the Stop receiving option.\par
\par
\b\fs28\par
Non RigCtl Transmitters\par
\b0\fs24 You can use other transceivers that aren\rquote t in the RigCtl database. To disable the Rigctl daemon select the \ldblquote Rigctl rigs\rdblquote  tab and check the box \ldblquote Do not use rigctld\'85\rdblquote . \par
If you do this you will need to manually select the transmit/receive frequencies and can use VOX to activate the transmitter. You will need to select the frequency manually in WSPR Scheduler for recording purposes \endash  so that you don\rquote t upload the wrong band to WSPRnet for example. You can do this by selecting the frequency from the list in the Slots or the TX Configuration tab.\par
However if your transmitter accepts command line parameters using a serial interface or a web based connection you can automate the frequency selection and transmission. To do this select the \ldblquote\b non rigctl TX\b0\rdblquote  tab. \par
To add a new TX select Add (and you can edit existing ones with Edit) \endash  give the new TX an appropriate name.\par
\b Serial connection\par
\b0 If your TX accepts a serial connection locate the COM port to which it is connected and configure the baud rate, data bits, parity, stop bits and flow control appropriate to your transmitter. Then in \ldblquote TX command\rdblquote  enter the command that will configure your WSPR TX. For example a particular WSPR TX uses a command sequence such as:\par
\b CONFIG:call,grid,pwr,freq\par
\b0 The callsign, locator, power (in dBm) and frequency (in Hz) will be modified according to the values selected in your slot database (or those selected from the TX Config tab if you have disabled the scheduler (by checking \ldblquote Disable sked\rdblquote ). Ie. you don\rquote t need to put actual values in \endash  just the corresponding variables that will be substituted at TX time. If a separate line is need to start the transmitter then enter this into the \ldblquote Command to start TX\rdblquote  box \endash  or just re-enter the same line again. If your TX requires a command to stop transmission enter this in the RX command below (or leave it blank if the TX stops itself).\par
\b TCP or UDP Connection\par
\b0 If the transmitter has a TCP (or UDP) connection you can enter its IP address and port number (if it is using a web based interface then it is normally TCP port 80, but you can change it in your TX software).\par
The external TX should have a web based Application Program Interface (API). You will need to enter the command that calls the API. Normally it starts with \ldblquote http//<ip address>:<port no>/. You only need to add the rest of the command to call the API commands in the \ldblquote API URL\rdblquote  text box. An example of this is: \ldblquote\b api/cmd?c=\b0\rdblquote . When combined with the header the call to the API becomes:\tab {\b\lang1033{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://<ip"}}{\fldrslt{\ul\cf1\b0 Error! Hyperlink reference not valid.}}}}\b\f0\fs24  \b0 address>:<port no>/api/cmd?c=\rdblquote\par
The header, including the, IP address and port number, will be automatically entered for you from the information you entered in the protocol, IP and port boxes above. An example output to the TX might be:  {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK http://192.168.1.2:80/api/cmd?c= }}{\fldrslt{http://192.168.1.2:80/api/cmd?c=\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24\rdblquote\par
Next in the \ldblquote TX command\rdblquote  box you enter the commands that will change the TX frequency and any other commands, separated by a semi colon.\par
For example:\par
\b SET FREQ freq;SET CALL call;SET OFFSET offset;SET LOC grid;SET PWR pwr\b0\par
These values (eg. freq, offset etc.) will be substituted for the values taken from the slot database (or if not using that from the TX Config tab) \endash  of course the call and locator will be those set in the TX Config tab. Note that the power setting does not modify the TX power in most cases \endash  you should set this to the power that your TX uses (ie. if you set the pwr to 40 and your WSPR TX amp is a 1 Watt amp \endash  you won\rquote t get 10 Watts out of it! \endash  you\rquote ll need to set this value to match the amp). Also note that if the TX has a GPS module it may override the locator (grid) setting.\par
In the \ldblquote Command to start TX\rdblquote  box you should then enter the API command that starts the WSPR transmission. For example: \b TXNOW\par
\b0 In the \ldblquote RX Command\rdblquote  box enter the command to return to receive. For example: \b STOP\par
Arduino Nano + AD9850 WSPR TX Exciter project\par
\b0 Note: the above commands have been tested with an Arduino Nano ESP32 driving an AD9850 DDS module to generate a WSPR transmission. A NEO-6M GPS module is used to provide accurate locator information. A diagram of this is shown in the \ldblquote Arduino_WSPR_TX\rdblquote  folder on Sourceforge where you will find a diagram of the WSPR TX exciter and Arduino source code. This software when uploaded to an Arduino Nano ESP32 has a serial interface, a web interface that can be accessed from a web browser and an API that can be used by third party software such as WSPR Scheduler to control the WSPR TX.\par
A separate user document will be available to set this up.\par
Once you have connected your Arduino WSPR TX to wifi you can then setup WSPR Scheduler under the Non rigctl tab as follows:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 a)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pnlcltr{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa120\sl276\slmult1 Add a new TX\par
{\pntext\f0 b)\tab}Enter a name (eg. \ldblquote WSPR nano AD9850 TX\rdblquote )\par
{\pntext\f0 c)\tab}Select TCP\par
{\pntext\f0 d)\tab}Enter the IP address of your TX module\par
{\pntext\f0 e)\tab}Enter port 80\par
{\pntext\f0 f)\tab}In the API URL filed enter: \tab\b api/cmd?c=\b0\par
{\pntext\f0 g)\tab}In the TX command field enter:\tab\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa120\sl276\slmult1\b SET FREQ freq;SET CALL call;SET OFFSET offset;SET LOC grid;SET PWR pwr\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa120\sl276\slmult1\b0 h)\tab In the Command to start TX filed enter:\tab\b TXNOW\b0\par
i)\tab In the RX Command field enter:\tab\b STOP\b0\par
j)\tab Hit Save to save the settings.\par
k)\tab To use the new TX double click on it in the list so that it becomes the selected rig.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sl276\slmult1 l)\tab From the \ldblquote Rigctl rigs\rdblquote  tab check the box \ldblquote Do not use rigctld\'85\rdblquote\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl276\slmult1 You can now use the TX.\par
\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b\fs28\par
Remote Control\par
\b0\fs24 The \ldblquote Remote\rdblquote  tab allows you to enable or disable transmit in WSPR Scheduler. \par
If you operate your WSPR beacon away from your station you must be able to disable the transmitter remotely. You would normally do this using a remote console system (eg. Remote Desktop, TightVNC, VNCViewer, Teamviewer, etc.), however a much simpler method of doing this is to use the Remote tab.\par
You can access this function from a browser on a PC, tablet or smartphone. This feature is useful if you need to disable the transmit function, for example if you plan to start portable operation. \par
You can also remotely enable or disable the WSPR Schedules and also remotely select a WSPR frequency and antenna. If TX is enabled you can start a WSPR transmission remotely on the chosen frequency or, if TX is disabled, receive transmissions. \par
To enable remote access:\par
1)\tab Select the Remote tab\par
2)\tab Check the Enable remote access checkbox\par
3)\tab If you want to enable remote operation of WSPR Scheduler for transmit and receive as well then check the \ldblquote Allow remote TX/RX control\rdblquote  checkbox.\par
4)\tab Enter a port number to use for remote operation \endash  for example 5029\par
5)\tab Enter a username for remote access\par
6)\tab Enter a password\par
7)\tab Enter a passcode \endash  this can be numeric\par
8)\tab Hit Save\par
You will then be able to remotely access the TX/RX function\par
Testing:\par
1)\tab Find the IP address of the PC running WSPR Scheduler by running the IPCONFIG command from the Command Prompt.\par
2)\tab On the same or another PC/smartphone/tablet on the local network open a browser\par
3)\tab Enter: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://<ip }}{\fldrslt{https://<ip\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24  address>:<port>\tab\tab - for example {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://192.168.10.2:5029 }}{\fldrslt{https://192.168.10.2:5029\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24\par
4)\tab Ignore any warnings (you don\rquote t have a recognised certificate installed so your browser will warn you) \endash  and open the page. \par
For example in the Firefox browser you should select Advanced when the warning appears and then Proceed to <IP address>:<port>\par
5)\tab When prompted to login enter the username, password and passcode\par
6)\tab You can then select one of the following functions:\par
i)\tab Status\tab - to check the current status (eg. TX enabled/disabled etc)\par
ii)\tab Enable or Disable TX\par
iii)\tab Disable or Enable sked (this will stop WSPR Scheduler from cycling through the prepared schedule of WSPR TX/RX slots)\par
7)\tab If you want to change the WSPR TX/RX frequency \endash  select it from the list\par
8)\tab If you want to change antenna \endash  select this from the list.\par
9)\tab If you want to start a WSPR transmission on the frequency/antenna then select \ldblquote Go next slot\rdblquote  \endash  this will automatically disable the schedule ready for the next WSPR timeslot\par
If the TX is enabled WSPR scheduler will transmit on the chosen frequency/antenna, but if disabled it will receive and decode transmissions.\par
Once you have finished with your session select Log out.\par
\par
\b Configuring a Static IP for the WSPR PC\par
\b0 It is best to configure a static IP address for the PC running WSPR Scheduler. You can either do this from the PC itself, from Network Connections, or by configuring a reserved IP on your router. How to do this depends on your router but, look for DHCP under advanced settings on your router and you should see an Address Reservation option. Select the PC (look for its current IP address) and then reserve its current one.\par
\b Accessing Externally\par
\b0 Your router will have a publicly visible IP address, but this may change unless your Internet Service Provider has allocated a static IP address to it \endash  this is something that you will have to ask your ISP to provide, usually at a small extra cost, but there are other techniques to keep track of a dynamic IP address, such as Dynamic DNS.\par
There are two methods to access the WSPR PC:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Address mapping\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 This will map your router external IP address (allocated by the ISP) to your internal WSPR PC address and port number. https should provide some security to the connection.\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 2.\tab Using a VPN\b\par

\pard\widctlpar\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1\b0 This will allow access to a VPN tunnel into your network \endash  once connected you can access your WSPR PC by its internal IP address and port number. This is a more secure option since the VPN tunnel is encrypted. This will provide additional security as well as https.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Please see the separate document: \b Remote_Control_Configuration_Vx.pdf\b0  on Sourceforge for further details on address mapping, VPN configuration and dynamic DNS\par
\b\fs32\page WSPR Scheduler Live\par
\b0\fs24 This is a separate app that retrieves reports of your WSPR transmission that have been uploaded to the WSPRnet web site and relayed via the WSPR live web site.\par
The database should be installed before installing this module. WSPR Scheduler should also be installed and transmission started so that reports of your transmission can be uploaded to WSPRnet/WSPR Live by other stations.\par
\b Installing WSPR Sked Live\par
\b0 Locate the WSPRlive msi file and install it with administrator permissions.\par
This will locate your call and start retrieving reports from other stations about your WSPR transmissions.\par
For each report it will display:\par
Date/time, your call, frequency received on, Signal to Noise ratio (SNR) of the received signal, TX drift observed, TX location, power in dBm, reporter call or id, reporter location, distance between TX-RX in km and miles, azimuth to the reporter and the decoder software version.\cf2\highlight3\f2  \cf0\highlight0\f0 It also includes an estimation of the likely strength of CW and SSB transmissions using higher power from your station, based on the reported signal strength of your WSPR transmissions.\par
Updates will be automatically retrieved from wspr.live every 6 minutes and will retrieve 10 minutes of reports, but this can be changed up to 24hours from the list box to the bottom left.\par
The data is recorded in the wspr_rx database (in the reported table)\par
Reception can be filtered by date, band, callsign (it accepts wildcards such as VK*), and by distance between a minimum and maximum. It also allows you to change the CW/SSB power level used estimating the reception quality of CW/SSB signals from your station, based on the SNR from reporters of your WSPR transmissions.\~\par
The filter can be applied using the Apply button and also cleared using the Clear button.\par
An update can be forced using the Update now button, but you will be prevented from pressing it again within a two-minute period.\par
\par
\b\page\fs32 WSPR Scheduler Map\par
\b0\fs24 The transmitted and received signals from/to your station can be viewed on a map that displays the output of the WSPR Scheduler receive window and WSPR Scheduler Live app. \par
You will need to have installed WSPR Scheduler and WSPR Scheduler Live as well as let them run for a few minutes before the map will start to be populated.\par
The options that are available are:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1)\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta)}}
\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa160\sl276\slmult1 Paths \endash  this is box is checked at startup and allows a line to be drawn between own station location and the location of reporters of own transmissions and also the location of WSPR transmissions received. \par
{\pntext\f0 2)\tab}TX and RX \endash  shown both transmitted and received WSPR transmission, TX only (reports of own call) or RX only (received WSPR calls).\par
{\pntext\f0 3)\tab}Don\rquote t show special calls \endash  sometimes balloon borne telemetry transmitters use WSPR to transmit data, however they often use the locator field in the WSPR transmission to send telemetry data instead \endash  so that the position information is often wildly inaccurate. Balloon callsigns often begin with Q, 0 or 1 \endash  if so these can be filtered out, although if they don\rquote t inaccurate positions may still appear on the map.\par
{\pntext\f0 4)\tab}The autotrack feature allows the map to be updated every few minutes.\par
{\pntext\f0 5)\tab}There is a band filter that allows only reports on a particular WSPR band to be displayed.\par
{\pntext\f0 6)\tab}The age of reports from the current time can also be selected (from 10 minutes to 2 days).\par
{\pntext\f0 7)\tab}Since there may be many points and track near to the own station origin on the map it is possible to filter out stations close to your own position from 0 to 1800 miles (or 0-2900 km).\par
{\pntext\f0 8)\tab}Once the options have been selected the filters chosen can be applied with the Apply button and cleared using the same button.\par
{\pntext\f0 9)\tab}You can zoom in and out using the mouse and select Recentre to go back to the original size and location.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\page\b\fs32 WSPR Scheduler Solar (WS_Solar)\par
\b0\fs24 This retrieves solar geomagnetic data from NOAA in Boulder, Colorado and its two GOES satellites. It can also display a daily propagation forecast from hamqsl.com courtesy of N0NBH. \par
The wspr databases must be installed before WSPR Scheduler Solar can be run, though it doesn\rquote t need any of the other apps to be running to work.\par
To install download the WSPR_Solar msi and install it, ideally with administrator permissions.\par
When you run it \endash  it will start downloading, recording and displaying the solar data.\par
The information provided and the rationale behind it are presented in the document "\b About_WS_Solar_vx.pdf\b0 " on Sourceforge.\par
