\fs24 Installing and using MySQL Community\par
\b0 It is recommend to use Xampp to run the MySQL databases - the Xampp Control panel allows easy access to the administration of the database files. The MariaDVB version of MySQL seems to less memory intensive too. Although Xampp requires a few steps to import the databases and configure the admin user and password.\line Both Xampp and MySQL use port 3306 when running, therefore both should not be running on port 3306 at the same time - you really need one or the other to be installed rather than both.\line However MySQL can be used as an alternative to using Xampp to host the databases. Configuration may be a little more complex, so an exe file has been created for this purpose.\line 1) The easiest way to install MySQL Community is to download and run the run the "wspr_mysql_setup,exe" from\~{{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://sourceforge.net/projects/wspr-scheduler/files/ }}{\fldrslt{https://sourceforge.net/projects/wspr-scheduler/files/\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs24\line 2) Run this and provide administrator privileges if prompted.\line 3) An installation will be created in the Program Files directory called "WSInstaller" (an uninstaller in there will remove the installer files (not MySQL) once you are finished with it)\par
The batch file will run the installer that will download MySQL Community and install it. It will then start the MySQL service (MySQL) and then import the WSPR Scheduler databases and add the admin user with the default password of wspr.\par
\line You are then ready to install WSPR Scheduler and the associated apps if you already haven't done so.\par
